An app-drawn title bar (ngterm's own on Windows, `titlebar.rs`): GPUI's Windows windows leave `start_window_move` and
`show_window_menu` to the trait's no-op defaults, so a bar drawn in a window whose system title bar is hidden
(`appears_transparent`) could neither move the window nor open its window menu. `start_window_move` posts the system's
drag move (`SC_MOVE | HTCAPTION`), `show_window_menu` posts a message of its own whose handler shows the system menu at
that point, its items enabled for the window's state, and runs the command picked; both are posted so the modal loops
run after the event that asked for them, outside GPUI's borrows; a fullscreen window does not move. With the title bar
hidden the top sizing border overlaps the client area, where the app's bar would take the press: a press on any sizing
border now goes to the system unseen by GPUI. A non-client press GPUI takes (a window control the app draws over
`WindowControlArea`) captures the mouse, as a client press does, so its release comes back wherever it lands.
scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified crates.io archive.

